<config>
  <!--
    Addresses are pinned explicitly, one host at a time. This looks verbose
    next to a wildcard like 192.168.x.x, but the wildcard form does not work
    here and the reason is worth knowing.

    IPv4NetworkConfigurator decides subnets by walking the topology, and it
    does not recognise an OpenFlow switch as a bridge. It therefore treats
    every host-to-switch cable as its own link and hands each one a separate
    /30 - so the hosts end up on 192.168.0.1/30, .5/30, .9/30 and cannot reach
    each other even though a layer-2 switch sits between them. Constraining the
    mask instead makes it fail outright with "failed to find address prefix",
    because it still wants one subnet per link.

    (The model ships modifiedInetFiles/openflow-inet-hack.patch to teach the
    configurator about OpenFlow switches, but it targets INET 2.x paths and
    does not apply to 3.8.5. Explicit addresses avoid needing it.)

    Fixed addresses also make the logs and the feature CSV readable: anything
    from 192.168.1.2x is an attacker, by construction.
  -->

  <!--
    All data-plane hosts share 192.168.1.0/24. The host part is left as 'x' so
    the configurator picks it: writing one rule per host with a literal address
    does not work, because the hosts='**.normalHost[0]' index is not matched
    per-instance and every client ends up claiming the same address.

    Hosts are therefore identified in results by module path
    (normalHost[i] / attacker[i] / victim), not by address.
  -->
  <interface hosts='**.normalHost[*] **.attacker[*] **.victim'
             names='eth0'
             address='192.168.1.x' netmask='255.255.255.0'/>

  <!-- control plane: switch to controller, kept off the data-plane subnet -->
  <interface hosts='**' names='eth0' address='10.x.x.x' netmask='255.x.x.x'/>
</config>
